-- title:
--   Residue disc of a point: sections, valuations, locality
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field, $A \subseteq L$ a valuation subring, $F$ a field extension of $L$, and $S \subseteq F$ a subring that is a local ring. The data are: a ring homomorphism $\iota : A \to S$ with $\iota(a) = a$ in $F$ for all $a \in A$; a ring homomorphism $\chi_0 : S \to A/\mathfrak m_A$ with $\chi_0 \circ \iota$ the residue map of $A$ and with $\mathfrak m_S = \ker \chi_0$; an element $t \in S$; and a valuation subring $B \subseteq F$. Write $\mathfrak q$ for the ideal of $S$ generated by $\iota(\mathfrak m_A)$. The hypotheses are: every $f \in F$ is of the form $g/h$ with $g, h \in S$, $h \neq 0$; $\operatorname{algebraMap} L F (c) \in B \iff c \in A$; $S \subseteq B$; every $f \in S \setminus \mathfrak q$ is non-zero with $f^{-1} \in B$; every $f \in B$ is $g/h$ with $g, h \in S$ and $h \notin \mathfrak q$; $\mathfrak q$ is prime and $t \notin \mathfrak q$; every non-zero $f \in F$ becomes a unit of $B$ after multiplication by some non-zero constant from $L$; (Hensel sections) for each $c \in A$ with residue $0$ there is exactly one ring homomorphism $\chi : S \to A$ with $\chi \circ \iota = \mathrm{id}$, residue of $\chi(f)$ equal to $\chi_0(f)$ for all $f$, and $\chi(t) = c$; (kernels) any such $\chi$ (with arbitrary $\chi(t)$) has $\ker \chi = (t - \iota(\chi(t)))$; (Weierstrass preparation) every $h \in S \setminus \mathfrak q$ equals a unit of $S$ times a finite product $\prod_i (t - \iota(r_i))$ with $r_i \in \mathfrak m_A$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a set $D$ of places $P$ of $F$ over $L$ (valuation subrings of $F$ containing $L$, proper, with principal ideals) such that: (i) $P \in D$ if and only if $\operatorname{algebraMap} L$ into the residue field of $P$ is surjective, every $f \in S$ lies in $\mathcal O_P$ with $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f) \in A$, and for $f \in S$ one has $A.\mathrm{valuation}(P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)) < 1$ exactly when $\chi_0(f) = 0$; (ii) each $\chi$ as above with $\chi \circ \iota = \mathrm{id}$ and residue compatibility is realised by a unique $P \in D$ with $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f) = \chi(f)$ for all $f \in S$; (iii) for $P \in D$, $\mathcal O_P$ consists exactly of the fractions $g/h$ with $g,h \in S$ and $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(h) \neq 0$; (iv) a non-zero $f \in F$ with $P.\mathrm{ord}(f) = 0$ for all $P \in D$ satisfies $c f \in S^\times$ for some non-zero $c \in L$; and (v) any $f \in B$ lying in $\mathcal O_P$ for all $P \in D$ lies in $S$.
--
--   This is the residue-disc (formal fibre) description of the local ring at a point of a curve over a henselian, not necessarily discrete, valuation ring: the disc is parametrised by $t$, its points correspond to the local sections $\chi$ of $S$ over $A$, valuations along the disc are read off from $\chi_0$, and functions integral on $B$ with no pole on the disc are regular. It is used in the construction of the smooth-point package for a proper model, via [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_smoothPointPackage_localRing_of_mem_smoothLocus_of_isProper`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_smoothPointPackage_localRing_of_mem_smoothLocus_of_isProper).
